      "math_academy_id": "e-11048",
      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y) =\\ln(y-x^2),$ evaluate $f_x(1,9).$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot 0-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,9)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,9) & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{9-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
